(function () {
  var m = {"IND386": {"abstract": "Bristol Myers Squibb is scaling AI from targeted experiments to enterprise transformation through its AI Accelerator program. In this session, hear how BMS pairs business-led innovation with AWS to move AI use cases from idea into production. See how this approach is applied across Product Development and Supply, where AI helps improve decision velocity, strengthen supply resilience, optimize operations, and speed the delivery of medicines to patients. Learn how BMS and AWS built the foundation, operating model, and reusable capabilities to scale AI responsibly, turning isolated wins into a repeatable enterprise capability that other organizations can follow.", "language": "en"},"IND387": {"abstract": "Developing a new medicine takes 10 to 15 years and $2.6 billion, and timelines are getting longer. Novartis decided not to optimize the old model but to replace it, building an AI-native ecosystem spanning information, decision, and physical AI across the drug development lifecycle. In this session, hear how AI now writes over 80% of their RWE protocols in production, then go deep on intelligent decision systems that optimize clinical trial planning, and formulation optimisations that improve the medicines Novartis creates. Learn how Novartis and AWS are compressing the path from AI prototype to production, turning ambition into patient impact.", "language": "en"},"IND389": {"abstract": "Physical AI — AI that senses, reasons, and acts in the physical world — is reshaping medical devices. From surgical robots to connected infusion pumps, imaging to wearables, every next-gen device needs training, simulation, edge deployment, and fleet management at scale. In this chalk talk, we’ll architect the full Physical AI stack on AWS for medical devices — SageMaker for model training, IoT Greengrass for edge inference, regulated OTA at fleet scale, and NVIDIA solutions integrated into the AWS backbone. We’ll whiteboard the hard tradeoffs — edge vs. cloud, regulatory constraints, and scaling from prototype to 100K devices. Leave with a reference architecture and a clear path to pilot", "language": "en"},"IND390": {"abstract": "Your clinical team wants an AI agent that summarizes patient notes—but one leaked SSN means a $1.5M HIPAA penalty. In this session, we build a working Amazon Bedrock Agent with HIPAA controls baked in. Guardrails automatically redact low-risk identifiers and block critical PHI, routing flagged content through Amazon EventBridge to an SQS review queue. AWS CloudTrail captures the full audit trail, and IAM restricts the agent to BAA-covered services only. You leave with a deployable CDK stack ready for production.", "language": "en"},"IND391": {"abstract": "This chalk talk explores how to architect agentic AI workflows for generative protein design at enterprise scale. Using Bayer's oncology nanobody campaign as the reference implementation, we walk through the technical and scientific decisions behind a pipeline that generated 50,000 candidates and 3,000 prioritized candidates in weeks using Amazon Bio Discovery. Topics include: model orchestration patterns for generative and predictive models, designing scoring functions that encode biological mechanism (not just binding affinity), iterative feedback loops between computation and wet-lab validation, and scaling considerations for enterprise drug discovery programs. Attendees will leave this session with a reusable architectural blueprint for building agentic biomolecule design workflows on AWS.", "language": "en"},"IND392": {"abstract": "\"Auto-scaling was only the beginning. At Roblox, scaling means continuously adapting to changing capacity, hardware availability, GPU constraints, and unpredictable global demand. Join Roblox to learn how they combine AWS with on-premises infrastructure to dynamically place workloads across regions, architectures, and AI providers. Using EC2 Capacity Health Visualizer, hybrid scheduling, multi-region load balancing, self-hosted AI inference alongside Amazon Bedrock, and heterogeneous compute (x86, Graviton, Inferentia, Trainium, GPUs), you will learn how Roblox keeps gaming and AI services responsive under record-breaking load.\"", "language": "en"},"IND394": {"abstract": "Large language models transformed text. Large transaction models (LTMs) aim to do the same for payment and commerce data. Instead of brittle, hand-crafted feature pipelines feeding hundreds of specialized models, LTMs are transformer-based foundation models pre-trained directly on billions of anonymized transactions, learning rich representations of transactions, merchant behavior, consumer activity, and financial risk. This session introduces Mastercard's LTM program, the architectural choices behind it, and the unique challenges of tokenizing and training on tabular and sequential financial data at massive scale. We show how foundation models power diverse production use cases, from fraud detection to personalization to reaching underserved cardholders.", "language": "en"},"IND397-R": {"abstract": "Agencies, the industrial base, and technology partners want agentic AI in production, but few have navigated the path across classification levels. In this chalk talk, learn real-world patterns for taking Bedrock agents and foundation models from prototype to authorized production across commercial and AWS Government Regions (FedRAMP, DoD CSP SRG IL2/4/5, classified) on one portable security baseline, without a rebuild; what is authorized at each level; the tradeoffs at each level; and ATO strategies that turn guardrails, observability, and evaluations into control evidence your assessor can use. Guardrails and audit-trail demos. Ideal for architects and security leads.", "language": "en"},"IND397-R1": {"abstract": "Every agency wants GenAI in production, but few have navigated the path across classification levels. In this Chalk Talk, learn real-world patterns for deploying Bedrock agents and foundation models across Government Regions - service parity, ATO strategies, and tradeoffs. Ideal for architects.", "language": "en"},"IND398": {"abstract": "AWS built STORMS with the Air Force SOC to defend DoD workloads across AWS accounts in multiple Organizations - pairing centralized security telemetry and one-click containment with a multi-agent Amazon Bedrock system that analyzes logs, answers analyst questions, and executes containment actions.", "language": "en"},"IND401-R": {"abstract": "Commissioning industrial robots typically takes weeks of physical testing. Digital twins with real-time physics simulation and multi-agent AI optimization can compress that timeline from weeks to hours. In this builders session, construct a digital twin of a multi-robot manufacturing cell using virtual PLCs and controllers, real fieldbus connectivity, real-time physics simulation in NVIDIA Omniverse, and Amazon EC2 as the compute backbone. Leave with an AI-optimized robotic program validated against real-world conditions before ever touching real hardware. Generated in 60 minutes using open standards and open toolchains, the result is a reusable blueprint that scales across cells, lines, and sites.", "language": "en"},"IND401-R1": {"abstract": "Commissioning industrial robots typically takes weeks of physical testing. Digital twins with real-time physics simulation and multi-agent AI optimization can compress that timeline from weeks to hours. In this builders session, construct a digital twin of a multi-robot manufacturing cell using virtual PLCs and controllers, real fieldbus connectivity, real-time physics simulation in NVIDIA Omniverse, and Amazon EC2 as the compute backbone. Leave with an AI-optimized robotic program validated against real-world conditions before ever touching real hardware. Generated in 60 minutes using open standards and open toolchains, the result is a reusable blueprint that scales across cells, lines, and sites.", "language": "en"},"IND402-R": {"abstract": "Manufacturing data lives in silos — from engineering systems through production lines. In this workshop, build an agentic digital thread: a knowledge-graph-powered AI system that unifies OT and IT data across the product lifecycle and answers cross-domain questions in real time. Using Amazon Neptune, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, and AWS IoT SiteWise, deploy agents that autonomously traverse connections from product design through production operations — linking engineering changes to equipment assets to quality events — without point-to-point integrations. Leave with a deployable reference architecture adaptable to any manufacturing environment.", "language": "en"},"IND402-R1": {"abstract": "Manufacturing data lives in silos — from engineering systems through production lines. In this workshop, build an agentic digital thread: a knowledge-graph-powered AI system that unifies OT and IT data across the product lifecycle and answers cross-domain questions in real time. Using Amazon Neptune, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, and AWS IoT SiteWise, deploy agents that autonomously traverse connections from product design through production operations — linking engineering changes to equipment assets to quality events — without point-to-point integrations. Leave with a deployable reference architecture adaptable to any manufacturing environment.", "language": "en"},"IND403": {"abstract": "This workshop tackles a question most media organizations have yet to ask: your video pipeline extracts rich metadata for human search and discovery, but is it actually optimized for agents? In this hands-on session, you will build the semantic infrastructure that bridges that gap: multimodal embeddings tuned for machine recall, hybrid search that combines vector similarity with keyword precision, and an entity graph that maps content relationships into structures agents can reason over. By the end of this workshop, you will have a media agent harness that queries, enriches, and continuously improves their video understanding, making video content not just searchable by people but optimized at scale for agents.", "language": "en"},"IND404-R": {"abstract": "The average US patient has records scattered across 40+ systems. Medication lists conflict, allergies are duplicated, and clinical notes contradict structured data. Every AI agent you connect downstream inherits that mess. In this builder session, you use AWS HealthLake to resolve patient identities across fragmented sources, normalize clinical concepts to SNOMED/LOINC/RxNorm, and produce a unified, queryable FHIR record. Then you use a unified SDK to wire Amazon Connect Health agents to your clean data layer, simulate a patient encounter, and generate a structured clinical note that writes back to HealthLake. You leave with a deployable data foundation that accelerates Connect Health agent integration today and supports any custom agent you choose to connect tomorrow.", "language": "en"},"IND404-R1": {"abstract": "The average US patient has records scattered across 40+ systems. Medication lists conflict, allergies are duplicated, and clinical notes contradict structured data. Every AI agent you connect downstream inherits that mess. In this builder session, you use AWS HealthLake to resolve patient identities across fragmented sources, normalize clinical concepts to SNOMED/LOINC/RxNorm, and produce a unified, queryable FHIR record. Then you use a unified SDK to wire Amazon Connect Health agents to your clean data layer, simulate a patient encounter, and generate a structured clinical note that writes back to HealthLake. You leave with a deployable data foundation that accelerates Connect Health agent integration today and supports any custom agent you choose to connect tomorrow.", "language": "en"},"IND405-R": {"abstract": "With the growth of digital media, including streaming video and retail media, customers that operate advertising platforms need to buy, sell, deliver, and measure millions of ad transactions per second while meeting stringent latency requirements. In this chalk talk, you will learn how to run real-time bidding workloads with your AdTech partners on a dedicated network in the cloud using AWS RTB Fabric. You will learn how to setup, onboard, and integrate AWS RTB Fabric with your supply and demand-side-platforms. Additionally, you will learn about key features including the core network, modules, and pricing model of the service.", "language": "en"},"IND405-R1": {"abstract": "With the growth of digital media, including streaming video and retail media, customers that operate advertising platforms need to buy, sell, deliver, and measure millions of ad transactions per second while meeting stringent latency requirements. In this chalk talk, you will learn how to run real-time bidding workloads with your AdTech partners on a dedicated network in the cloud using AWS RTB Fabric. You will learn how to setup, onboard, and integrate AWS RTB Fabric with your supply and demand-side-platforms. Additionally, you will learn about key features including the core network, modules, and pricing model of the service.", "language": "en"},"IND406-R": {"abstract": "The restaurant industry is transforming as AI agents move from pilots to production. Leading QSR and casual dining brands are deploying a \"virtual general manager,\" multiple specialized agents orchestrated together to optimize drive-thru ordering, kitchen workflows, labor scheduling, and guest experience in real time. In this chalk talk, we whiteboard a multi-agent architecture using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and Strands Agents SDK, with Amazon Nova Sonic powering voice ordering at sub-2-second latency. We design a supervisor agent coordinating specialists across drive-thru, kitchen prep, inventory, and labor scheduling. Explore how agents share context, resolve conflicts, validate decisions against synthetic personas before deployment, and maintain human-in-the-loop oversight. Bring your architecture challenges.", "language": "en"},"IND406-R1": {"abstract": "The restaurant industry is transforming as AI agents move from pilots to production. Leading QSR and casual dining brands are deploying a \"virtual general manager,\" multiple specialized agents orchestrated together to optimize drive-thru ordering, kitchen workflows, labor scheduling, and guest experience in real time. In this chalk talk, we whiteboard a multi-agent architecture using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and Strands Agents SDK, with Amazon Nova Sonic powering voice ordering at sub-2-second latency. We design a supervisor agent coordinating specialists across drive-thru, kitchen prep, inventory, and labor scheduling. Explore how agents share context, resolve conflicts, validate decisions against synthetic personas before deployment, and maintain human-in-the-loop oversight. Bring your architecture challenges.", "language": "en"},"IND409-R": {"abstract": "Discover how the AWS Secure Research Environment (SRE) helps research institutions stand up NIST 800-171 and CMMC Level 2 infrastructure. Explore the architecture: Landing Zone Accelerator (LZA) customization, multi-account strategy with Control Tower Account Factory, preventive SCPs versus detective controls, Network Firewall egress, and automated evidence collection feeding Security Hub. No setup is required. Using a simulated pipeline, you work through and gain a deep understanding of the LZA configuration customizations an SRE needs, customize them for your organization, and leave with your own SRE config files. We provide next-step instructions for deploying those configs in a real, customer-owned AWS environment later.", "language": "en"},"IND409-R1": {"abstract": "Discover how the AWS Secure Research Environment (SRE) helps research institutions stand up NIST 800-171 and CMMC Level 2 infrastructure. Explore the architecture: Landing Zone Accelerator (LZA) customization, multi-account strategy with Control Tower Account Factory, preventive SCPs versus detective controls, Network Firewall egress, and automated evidence collection feeding Security Hub. No setup is required. Using a simulated pipeline, you work through and gain a deep understanding of the LZA configuration customizations an SRE needs, customize them for your organization, and leave with your own SRE config files. We provide next-step instructions for deploying those configs in a real, customer-owned AWS environment later.", "language": "en"},"IND410-R": {"abstract": "Federal dev teams struggle to ship fast while meeting compliance requirements. In this workshop, build a real app using Kiro's agentic IDE. Automate testing and docs with hooks, enforce AI behavior with steering rules, and extend capabilities through powers. Ideal for federal devs and architects.", "language": "en"},"IND410-R1": {"abstract": "Federal dev teams struggle to ship fast while meeting compliance requirements. In this workshop, build a real app using Kiro's agentic IDE. Automate testing and docs with hooks, enforce AI behavior with steering rules, and extend capabilities through powers. Ideal for federal devs and architects.", "language": "en"},"IND415": {"abstract": "In this hands-on workshop, build Agentic Real-Time Framework (ARTF)-compliant agents that operate inside the bidstream, using the Agents for Advertising framework on Amazon Bedrock. You'll construct an agent container that accepts bid requests and returns pricing decisions to the ARTF specification, then orchestrate a multi-agent pipeline that simulates adversarial auction scenarios and scores your bidding strategy against the outcomes. Run interactive simulations that generate learning signals, and plug in your own bidding model to see how it performs. You'll leave with a working ARTF container and auction-simulation harness you can point at your own models.", "language": "en"},"IND416": {"abstract": "Retail and CPG brands invest years building product expertise, from visual styling rules to ingredient pairing and formulation knowledge, that generic AI simply cannot replicate. In this builder session, you'll construct a Graph RAG solution that encodes brand-specific curatorial knowledge into an Amazon Neptune Analytics knowledge graph with multimodal vector embeddings. Using a CPG product catalog, you'll build graph schemas capturing formulation relationships, brand hierarchies, and cross-category pairing rules. Then see how vector similarity search combined with graph traversal enriches retrieval context before grounding Amazon Bedrock LLM responses. Leave with a working deployed architecture adaptable across retail and CPG.", "language": "en"},"IND417-R": {"abstract": "Video understanding unlocks powerful insights from media assets, but cost concerns often slow adoption. In this hands-on session, we walk through practical techniques for building cost-optimized video understanding pipelines on AWS. You'll learn how to reduce inference costs, right-size your architecture, and accelerate time to value without sacrificing accuracy. Whether you're processing thousands of hours of content for metadata enrichment, content moderation, or search, these patterns help you scale AI-driven video analysis while keeping your bill predictable.", "language": "en"},"IND417-R1": {"abstract": "Video understanding unlocks powerful insights from media assets, but cost concerns often slow adoption. In this hands-on session, we walk through practical techniques for building cost-optimized video understanding pipelines on AWS. You'll learn how to reduce inference costs, right-size your architecture, and accelerate time to value without sacrificing accuracy. Whether you're processing thousands of hours of content for metadata enrichment, content moderation, or search, these patterns help you scale AI-driven video analysis while keeping your bill predictable.", "language": "en"},"IND418-R": {"abstract": "Accelerate mission-critical federal operations with agentic AI—from public-information analysis to sensitive document workflows. This expert chalk talk explores patterns for document review, supply chain optimization and classified medical health records backed by real-world implementations. Whiteboard multi-agent orchestration with Amazon Bedrock, including least-privilege tool access, human approval, auditability, and failure recovery. Compare Amazon Bedrock AgentCore for eligible workloads such as regulatory review, grants, and benefits processing with FEDRAMP (Class C and D). Further explore container-based agent architectures for IL4/IL5 environments using authorized AWS services and models.", "language": "en"},"IND418-R1": {"abstract": "A federal agency achieved 3x faster document analysis using Agentic AI with autonomous reasoning. This chalk talk explores how AI agents orchestrate mission-critical tasks in high-side environments while meeting IL4/IL5 compliance and zero-trust requirements.", "language": "en"},"IND419": {"abstract": "More than half of web traffic now comes from AI bots that consume content built for humans while returning little value to publishers. This hands-on workshop tackles both the content and the monetization side. First, you'll build a pipeline that converts images, video, and audio into concise, agent-friendly markdown summaries including interpreting what's shown, said, and meant, while preserving the originals for human readers. Then you'll monetize bot traffic with AWS WAF AI traffic monetization, setting per-request pricing by bot verification tier and collecting payment via the x402 protocol. You'll build a working dual-audience pipeline and WAF pricing configuration you can deploy against your own media.", "language": "en"},"IND420-R": {"abstract": "Media operations teams struggle to correlate issues across complex live streaming pipelines. In this hands-on session, build an agentic video observability and analytics application using Kiro and Amazon Bedrock that monitors the entire live streaming chain using AWS Elemental MediaConnect, MediaLive, MediaPackage, MediaTailor and Amazon CloudFront. Deploy AI agents using Strands Agents SDK and MCP servers to ingest, visualize, and investigate end-to-end QoE/QoS analytics across three layers: origin health, CDN performance, and client-side CMCD telemetry. Learn how to create a unified dashboard and natural language interface for streaming quality investigation across every pipeline stage.", "language": "en"},"IND420-R1": {"abstract": "Media operations teams struggle to correlate issues across complex live streaming pipelines. In this hands-on session, build an agentic video observability and analytics application using Kiro and Amazon Bedrock that monitors the entire live streaming chain using AWS Elemental MediaConnect, MediaLive, MediaPackage, MediaTailor and Amazon CloudFront. Deploy AI agents using Strands Agents SDK and MCP servers to ingest, visualize, and investigate end-to-end QoE/QoS analytics across three layers: origin health, CDN performance, and client-side CMCD telemetry. Learn how to create a unified dashboard and natural language interface for streaming quality investigation across every pipeline stage.", "language": "en"},"IND421": {"abstract": "Walk through the reference architecture of a real-time fact-checking pipeline built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. See how a live audio stream is turned into fact-by-fact verdicts (true, misleading, or false), with source citing at broadcast-compatible latency. Learn how to build it yourself using a chain of specialized agents on AgentCore, hybrid search on Amazon OpenSearch Service, Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, and custom tool-use hooks. Leave with a mapped path to create a reference architecture, plus answers to the concrete design questions blocking you such as: agent sharding, tabular corpus indexing, guardrails, and streaming reasoning to the UI.", "language": "en"},"IND422": {"abstract": "Education institutions field thousands of student inquiries each semester across enrollment, financial aid, and academic support. In this chalk talk, we architect agentic AI-powered student services using Amazon Connect Customer. You’ll configure a knowledge base with university content and connect it to Connect Customer for real-time AI-generated recommendations during live calls, then deploy a self-service experience that resolves common inquiries over voice and chat. We'll cover when to use deterministic intent handling vs. agentic reasoning, MCP tool integration with student systems, and guardrails for FERPA compliance. By the end, you'll have a working prototype that boosts advisor productivity and reduces student wait times.", "language": "en"},"IND423": {"abstract": "State HHS agencies spend hundreds of millions maintaining eligibility rules engines built on Drools or proprietary tooling. These systems are brittle, expensive, and impossible to audit. We built a different path: Ingesting policy, using Large Language Models, and building a formal logic representation, followed by using neurosymbolic techniques to produce a verified rule set, enabling deterministic eligibility decisions with full audit trails. No hallucinations. Provable correctness. We walk through the architecture, show a working prototype processing SNAP and Medicaid policy, discuss pricing at scale, and share early results from state engagements.", "language": "en"},"INV001-R": {"abstract": "Ctrl+Alt+Ship is a choose-your-own-adventure experience with self-paced labs spanning Kiro, Amazon Quick, and Amazon Connect Customer. Start with the Kiro IDE for AI-native development, or level up to multi-agent workflows. Solve an escape-room-style corporate crisis using Quick, your AI work assistant, choosing between a guided investigation or an advanced multi-step operation to plan, execute, and automate your way to cracking the case. With Connect Customer, see how AI resolves customer issues, gives agents context, and helps supervisors improve service—or go hands-on with the agentic CX designer, a no-code visual canvas, to build conversational AI. AWS facilitators from each product team will be available to answer questions. Labs are 30-60 min. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed.", "language": "en"},"INV001-R1": {"abstract": "Ctrl+Alt+Ship is a choose-your-own-adventure experience with self-paced labs spanning Kiro, Amazon Quick, and Amazon Connect Customer. Start with the Kiro IDE for AI-native development, or level up to multi-agent workflows. Solve an escape-room-style corporate crisis using Quick, your AI work assistant, choosing between a guided investigation or an advanced multi-step operation to plan, execute, and automate your way to cracking the case. With Connect Customer, see how AI resolves customer issues, gives agents context, and helps supervisors improve service—or go hands-on with the agentic CX designer, a no-code visual canvas, to build conversational AI. AWS facilitators from each product team will be available to answer questions. Labs are 30-60 min. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed.", "language": "en"},"INV001-R2": {"abstract": "Ctrl+Alt+Ship is a choose-your-own-adventure experience with self-paced labs spanning Kiro, Amazon Quick, and Amazon Connect Customer. Start with the Kiro IDE for AI-native development, or level up to multi-agent workflows. Solve an escape-room-style corporate crisis using Quick, your AI work assistant, choosing between a guided investigation or an advanced multi-step operation to plan, execute, and automate your way to cracking the case. With Connect Customer, see how AI resolves customer issues, gives agents context, and helps supervisors improve service—or go hands-on with the agentic CX designer, a no-code visual canvas, to build conversational AI. AWS facilitators from each product team will be available to answer questions. Labs are 30-60 min. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed.", "language": "en"},"INV001-R3": {"abstract": "Ctrl+Alt+Ship is a choose-your-own-adventure experience with self-paced labs spanning Kiro, Amazon Quick, and Amazon Connect Customer. Start with the Kiro IDE for AI-native development, or level up to multi-agent workflows. Solve an escape-room-style corporate crisis using Quick, your AI work assistant, choosing between a guided investigation or an advanced multi-step operation to plan, execute, and automate your way to cracking the case. With Connect Customer, see how AI resolves customer issues, gives agents context, and helps supervisors improve service—or go hands-on with the agentic CX designer, a no-code visual canvas, to build conversational AI. AWS facilitators from each product team will be available to answer questions. Labs are 30-60 min. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed.", "language": "en"},"INV001-R4": {"abstract": "Ctrl+Alt+Ship is a choose-your-own-adventure experience with self-paced labs spanning Kiro, Amazon Quick, and Amazon Connect Customer. Start with the Kiro IDE for AI-native development, or level up to multi-agent workflows. Solve an escape-room-style corporate crisis using Quick, your AI work assistant, choosing between a guided investigation or an advanced multi-step operation to plan, execute, and automate your way to cracking the case. With Connect Customer, see how AI resolves customer issues, gives agents context, and helps supervisors improve service—or go hands-on with the agentic CX designer, a no-code visual canvas, to build conversational AI. AWS facilitators from each product team will be available to answer questions. Labs are 30-60 min. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed.", "language": "en"},"INV002-S-R": {"abstract": "Build and deploy a personalized eve agent with Vercel and AWS in this hands-on innovation lab. You'll start from zero, scaffold an open-source eve.dev agent framework, configure it with your own instructions, connect it to Amazon Bedrock through Vercel Al Gateway, and deploy it to Vercel. The lab is designed for a range of builders: beginners and non-technical users can quickly build and launch a functional agent, while more advanced engineers can explore how the same agent architecture can connect to AWS services and enterprise data sources such as Aurora, S3, or DynamoDB using Vercel and AWS native integration and advanced Vercel networking. Along the way, you'll learn how Vercel and AWS work together to help teams move from Al prototype to production-ready application. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed. Labs are 30-60 min. This presentation is brought to you by Vercel, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"INV002-S-R1": {"abstract": "Build and deploy a personalized eve agent with Vercel and AWS in this hands-on innovation lab. You'll start from zero, scaffold an open-source eve.dev agent framework, configure it with your own instructions, connect it to Amazon Bedrock through Vercel Al Gateway, and deploy it to Vercel. The lab is designed for a range of builders: beginners and non-technical users can quickly build and launch a functional agent, while more advanced engineers can explore how the same agent architecture can connect to AWS services and enterprise data sources such as Aurora, S3, or DynamoDB using Vercel and AWS native integration and advanced Vercel networking. Along the way, you'll learn how Vercel and AWS work together to help teams move from Al prototype to production-ready application. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed. Labs are 30-60 min. This presentation is brought to you by Vercel, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"INV003-S": {"abstract": "Claude Code is Anthropic's agentic coding tool. Point it at a codebase and choose your path, self-paced in the browser on Amazon Bedrock. In the Build with Claude track, give Claude project context, explore an unfamiliar repository, then plan, implement, and test a change you watch run - no experience required. In the Scale with Agents track, orchestrate parallel subagents across an agentic app, add review and verification gates, then apply Anthropic-authored skills to improve its performance and lower cost. Terminal experience assumed. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed. Labs are 30-60 min. This presentation is brought to you by Anthropic, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"INV301-S": {"abstract": "Go hands-on with OpenAI models on Amazon Bedrock across two builder tracks. First, stand up a fully managed autonomous agent with Amazon Bedrock Managed Agents: define instructions and tools, wire action groups for API access, and ground responses in your own data through knowledge bases. Then shift to code security, using OpenAI's Codex Security to review a real vulnerable app by running scans, threat modeling, triaging findings, and fixing one with verified remediation. Throughout, inference runs securely on Bedrock under your account's controls. You'll leave with repeatable blueprints for building agentic OpenAI applications and for AI-assisted application security on AWS. Workstations are provided; no reservation needed. Labs are 30-60 min. This presentation is brought to you by OpenAI, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"INV501": {"abstract": "Can an AI system move beyond answering questions to actually conducting research alongside you? We built one. AtoZ AI Co-Scientist is a multi-agent orchestration platform where specialized agents collaborate through a unified interface to discover literature, synthesize knowledge via graph-routed retrieval, generate hypotheses, implement baselines from papers, and run experiments autonomously. In this chalk talk, we dissect the compositional architecture, the graph-routed retrieval system achieving <200ms with structural signals flat search cannot capture, and Co-Scientist Bench—our agent-agnostic evaluation framework. Bring hard questions about reliability, memory, and human oversight.", "language": "en"},"INV501-R": {"abstract": "Can an AI system move beyond answering questions to actually conducting research alongside you? We built one. AtoZ AI Co-Scientist is a multi-agent orchestration platform where specialized agents collaborate through a unified interface to discover literature, synthesize knowledge via graph-routed retrieval, generate hypotheses, implement baselines from papers, and run experiments autonomously. In this chalk talk, we dissect the compositional architecture, the graph-routed retrieval system achieving <200ms with structural signals flat search cannot capture, and Co-Scientist Bench—our agent-agnostic evaluation framework. Bring hard questions about reliability, memory, and human oversight.", "language": "en"},"INV501-R1": {"abstract": "Can an AI system move beyond answering questions to actually conducting research alongside you? We built one. AtoZ AI Co-Scientist is a multi-agent orchestration platform where specialized agents collaborate through a unified interface to discover literature, synthesize knowledge via graph-routed retrieval, generate hypotheses, implement baselines from papers, and run experiments autonomously. In this chalk talk, we dissect the compositional architecture, the graph-routed retrieval system achieving <200ms with structural signals flat search cannot capture, and Co-Scientist Bench—our agent-agnostic evaluation framework. Bring hard questions about reliability, memory, and human oversight.", "language": "en"},"INV502": {"abstract": "It is possible to prove factual statements about systems to observers without revealing any details about those systems beyond the stated claims. This is surprising, but not as surprising as the fact that it is borderline practical using Zero Knowledge Proofs. This talk will give an overview of the mathematical foundations of Zero Knowledge Proofs and introduce both interactive and non-interactive proof schemes. It will then dive into the details of some of the current general purpose Zero Knowledge proof systems (e.g., zk-SNARK and zk-STARK) and show how to use Zero Knowledge libraries to attest to (or prove) properties of software systems without sharing the implementations of those systems.", "language": "en"},"INV502-R": {"abstract": "It is possible to prove factual statements about systems to observers without revealing any details about those systems beyond the stated claims. This is surprising, but not as surprising as the fact that it is borderline practical using Zero Knowledge Proofs. This talk will give an overview of the mathematical foundations of Zero Knowledge Proofs and introduce both interactive and non-interactive proof schemes. It will then dive into the details of some of the current general purpose Zero Knowledge proof systems (e.g., zk-SNARK and zk-STARK) and show how to use Zero Knowledge libraries to attest to (or prove) properties of software systems without sharing the implementations of those systems.", "language": "en"},"INV502-R1": {"abstract": "It is possible to prove factual statements about systems to observers without revealing any details about those systems beyond the stated claims. This is surprising, but not as surprising as the fact that it is borderline practical using Zero Knowledge Proofs. This talk will give an overview of the mathematical foundations of Zero Knowledge Proofs and introduce both interactive and non-interactive proof schemes. It will then dive into the details of some of the current general purpose Zero Knowledge proof systems (e.g., zk-SNARK and zk-STARK) and show how to use Zero Knowledge libraries to attest to (or prove) properties of software systems without sharing the implementations of those systems.", "language": "en"},"INV503": {"abstract": "As AI systems become increasingly capable and autonomous, safety evaluation is emerging as a critical challenge. While current evaluations largely rely on benchmark performance and empirical red-teaming, these approaches provide limited guarantees about real-world risk. This talk will explore an emerging direction in AI safety: evaluating model behavior with formal statistical guarantees. We will discuss how ideas from reliability engineering, robustness certification, and risk assessment can be applied to quantify catastrophic failure probabilities, the assumptions behind such guarantees, and their implications for deploying and governing frontier AI systems. This topic is highly relevant to the future of trustworthy and accountable AI.", "language": "en"},"INV503-R": {"abstract": "As AI systems become increasingly capable and autonomous, safety evaluation is emerging as a critical challenge. While current evaluations largely rely on benchmark performance and empirical red-teaming, these approaches provide limited guarantees about real-world risk. This talk will explore an emerging direction in AI safety: evaluating model behavior with formal statistical guarantees. We will discuss how ideas from reliability engineering, robustness certification, and risk assessment can be applied to quantify catastrophic failure probabilities, the assumptions behind such guarantees, and their implications for deploying and governing frontier AI systems. This topic is highly relevant to the future of trustworthy and accountable AI.", "language": "en"},"INV503-R1": {"abstract": "As AI systems become increasingly capable and autonomous, safety evaluation is emerging as a critical challenge. While current evaluations largely rely on benchmark performance and empirical red-teaming, these approaches provide limited guarantees about real-world risk. This talk will explore an emerging direction in AI safety: evaluating model behavior with formal statistical guarantees. We will discuss how ideas from reliability engineering, robustness certification, and risk assessment can be applied to quantify catastrophic failure probabilities, the assumptions behind such guarantees, and their implications for deploying and governing frontier AI systems. This topic is highly relevant to the future of trustworthy and accountable AI.", "language": "en"},"INV504": {"abstract": "S3 has a long history of using automated reasoning, but a core challenge has been applying these techniques to large systems while maintaining development velocity. Over time, we've learned to balance cutting-edge science with engineering velocity to accelerate development. We'll share diverse techniques for validating critical storage components, from black-box model-based validation using formal API specifications, to white-box verification with specialized concurrency checkers for ensuring transaction serializability and crash consistency. You'll learn how S3 engineers and scientists are advancing systems verification with new tools and ideas that you can apply to your own systems.", "language": "en"},"INV504-R": {"abstract": "S3 has a long history of using automated reasoning, but a core challenge has been applying these techniques to large systems while maintaining development velocity. Over time, we've learned to balance cutting-edge science with engineering velocity to accelerate development. We'll share diverse techniques for validating critical storage components, from black-box model-based validation using formal API specifications, to white-box verification with specialized concurrency checkers for ensuring transaction serializability and crash consistency. You'll learn how S3 engineers and scientists are advancing systems verification with new tools and ideas that you can apply to your own systems.", "language": "en"},"INV504-R1": {"abstract": "S3 has a long history of using automated reasoning, but a core challenge has been applying these techniques to large systems while maintaining development velocity. Over time, we've learned to balance cutting-edge science with engineering velocity to accelerate development. We'll share diverse techniques for validating critical storage components, from black-box model-based validation using formal API specifications, to white-box verification with specialized concurrency checkers for ensuring transaction serializability and crash consistency. You'll learn how S3 engineers and scientists are advancing systems verification with new tools and ideas that you can apply to your own systems.", "language": "en"},"INV505": {"abstract": "Large language models have shifted expectations about search: from retrieving documents to answering questions directly; from single-type data to multi-modal data; from one-shot lookup to multi-step reasoning. This is the new frontier of search empowered by AI. What should you know about the AI Search frontier? How can you make AI search provide the highest accuracy on your data, while keeping latency and costs in check? Join us to chart the course — and leave with a practical map.", "language": "en"},"INV506": {"abstract": "AI agents are probabilistic: vulnerable to prompt injection, hallucination, and unexpected tool composition. The controls around them must be deterministic. This session explores how to define provable safety envelopes for autonomous agents, drawing from the authorization and formal verification research behind AgentCore policy. We examine how automated reasoning guarantees policy correctness, how neuro-symbolic approaches combine LLM flexibility with mathematical verification for policy authoring, and how agents' action spaces can be reduced before execution. Attendees will also get a first look at new research extending these guarantees to emerging agentic authorization challenges.", "language": "en"},"INV506-R": {"abstract": "AI agents are probabilistic: vulnerable to prompt injection, hallucination, and unexpected tool composition. The controls around them must be deterministic. This session explores how to define provable safety envelopes for autonomous agents, drawing from the authorization and formal verification research behind AgentCore policy. We examine how automated reasoning guarantees policy correctness, how neuro-symbolic approaches combine LLM flexibility with mathematical verification for policy authoring, and how agents' action spaces can be reduced before execution. Attendees will also get a first look at new research extending these guarantees to emerging agentic authorization challenges.", "language": "en"},"INV507": {"abstract": "Automated theorem provers can reason symbolically about a system's behavior and prove properties like \"no user can ever escalate their own privileges\" across all possible inputs. Of course, this requires modeling your problem in first-order logic, which demands careful thinking to precisely capture all behaviors of your system. In this session, learn how to encode your problem in first-order logic: starting with a logic puzzle to build intuition, then a toy authorization language, and finally what it takes for a full production system like Cedar, a fine-grained authorization language built with high assurance. We discuss challenges including ensuring correctness and completeness of the encoding.", "language": "en"},"INV508": {"abstract": "Organizations want to automate enforcement of critical rules—financial, legal, regulatory—yet those rules live in ambiguous natural-language documents. Bridging this gap is error-prone and expensive. In this session, we show how neurosymbolic AI enables subject-matter experts to formalize natural-language rules into mathematical logic—capturing the original intent in an unambiguous, executable, and verifiable form. We cover the mathematical foundations and demonstrate through practical examples how formalized rules can serve as agent guardrails, compliance checks, and trusted knowledge sources. Attendees will leave with a deep understanding of how neurosymbolic techniques can help them formalize and operationalize their own domain rules.", "language": "en"},"INV508-R": {"abstract": "Organizations want to automate enforcement of critical rules—financial, legal, regulatory—yet those rules live in ambiguous natural-language documents. Bridging this gap is error-prone and expensive. In this session, we show how neurosymbolic AI enables subject-matter experts to formalize natural-language rules into mathematical logic—capturing the original intent in an unambiguous, executable, and verifiable form. We cover the mathematical foundations and demonstrate through practical examples how formalized rules can serve as agent guardrails, compliance checks, and trusted knowledge sources. Attendees will leave with a deep understanding of how neurosymbolic techniques can help them formalize and operationalize their own domain rules.", "language": "en"},"INV508-R1": {"abstract": "Organizations want to automate enforcement of critical rules—financial, legal, regulatory—yet those rules live in ambiguous natural-language documents. Bridging this gap is error-prone and expensive. In this session, we show how neurosymbolic AI enables subject-matter experts to formalize natural-language rules into mathematical logic—capturing the original intent in an unambiguous, executable, and verifiable form. We cover the mathematical foundations and demonstrate through practical examples how formalized rules can serve as agent guardrails, compliance checks, and trusted knowledge sources. Attendees will leave with a deep understanding of how neurosymbolic techniques can help them formalize and operationalize their own domain rules.", "language": "en"},"INV509": {"abstract": "Non-Uniform Memory Access (NUMA) topology shapes how every byte of model weight reaches a compute core, and misalignment silently costs throughput. This session explains why NUMA matters for memory locality when running models on EC2, and what changes across Graviton-, Intel-, and AMD-based instances. CPU inference requires deliberate alignment of threads to data, especially as SIMD and AMX instructions amplify memory bandwidth demands per core. Even on GPU instances, host-side NUMA affects preprocessing and KV cache offload. Understand your hardware topology and leverage it to get the most from your inference workloads.", "language": "en"},"INV510": {"abstract": "When Amazon S3 launched in 2006, its design requirements fit one page: scalable, reliable, fast, inexpensive, simple, with no single points of failure. The system was built to treat component failure as normal and use scale as an advantage. Today those founding principles describe an architecture serving hundreds of millions of requests per second across tens of millions of drives, maintaining eleven nines of durability while shipping features without downtime. Hear S3 principal engineers trace the decisions from single host to strong consistency at global scale, how formal methods verify correctness, and how the team balances innovation with operational safety.", "language": "en"},"INV510-R": {"abstract": "When Amazon S3 launched in 2006, its design requirements fit one page: scalable, reliable, fast, inexpensive, simple, with no single points of failure. The system was built to treat component failure as normal and use scale as an advantage. Today those founding principles describe an architecture serving hundreds of millions of requests per second across tens of millions of drives, maintaining eleven nines of durability while shipping features without downtime. Hear S3 principal engineers trace the decisions from single host to strong consistency at global scale, how formal methods verify correctness, and how the team balances innovation with operational safety.", "language": "en"},"INV510-R1": {"abstract": "When Amazon S3 launched in 2006, its design requirements fit one page: scalable, reliable, fast, inexpensive, simple, with no single points of failure. The system was built to treat component failure as normal and use scale as an advantage. Today those founding principles describe an architecture serving hundreds of millions of requests per second across tens of millions of drives, maintaining eleven nines of durability while shipping features without downtime. Hear S3 principal engineers trace the decisions from single host to strong consistency at global scale, how formal methods verify correctness, and how the team balances innovation with operational safety.", "language": "en"},"INV511": {"abstract": "What does a kernel language need to unlock peak performance on Trainium, AWS purpose built AI chips to deliver better price performance? The AWS Neuron Kernel Interface (NKI) is a Python-embedded DSL that goes beyond framework-level abstractions, giving developers direct access to Trainium's three-level memory hierarchy and four compute engines. Explore the design choices that enable hardware-level optimization, dissect production kernels powering FlashAttention variants, MoE routing, and on-device collectives, and debate where tile-based languages converge or diverge across accelerator architectures. Discover how NKI extends to support MXFP8 and disaggregated inference, and leave equipped to write custom, hardware-optimized kernels.", "language": "en"},"INV512": {"abstract": "What formal properties must a software representation satisfy for agents to manage entire application lifecycles autonomously — reasoning at the specification level, not the code level? This session examines the open scientific questions behind agentic code understanding: encoding both structural topology and business semantics into a unified knowledge graph, extracting and grounding business rules when institutional context has been lost, enabling agent reasoning over large heterogeneous graphs without coherence degradation, and establishing deterministic validation signals that replace human judgment with formal guarantees. Drawing from graph theory, knowledge representation, specification mining, and formal verification research, we explore what makes these problems hard, where current approaches break at scale, and what architectural principles make full-lifecycle autonomy tractable. Attendees will leave with a rigorous understanding of the representation and reasoning foundations required for agents that don’t just generate code but understand, validate, evolve, and replace software as knowledge.", "language": "en"},"INV512-R": {"abstract": "What formal properties must a software representation satisfy for agents to manage entire application lifecycles autonomously — reasoning at the specification level, not the code level? This session examines the open scientific questions behind agentic code understanding: encoding both structural topology and business semantics into a unified knowledge graph, extracting and grounding business rules when institutional context has been lost, enabling agent reasoning over large heterogeneous graphs without coherence degradation, and establishing deterministic validation signals that replace human judgment with formal guarantees. Drawing from graph theory, knowledge representation, specification mining, and formal verification research, we explore what makes these problems hard, where current approaches break at scale, and what architectural principles make full-lifecycle autonomy tractable. Attendees will leave with a rigorous understanding of the representation and reasoning foundations required for agents that don’t just generate code but understand, validate, evolve, and replace software as knowledge.", "language": "en"},"INV512-R1": {"abstract": "What formal properties must a software representation satisfy for agents to manage entire application lifecycles autonomously — reasoning at the specification level, not the code level? This session examines the open scientific questions behind agentic code understanding: encoding both structural topology and business semantics into a unified knowledge graph, extracting and grounding business rules when institutional context has been lost, enabling agent reasoning over large heterogeneous graphs without coherence degradation, and establishing deterministic validation signals that replace human judgment with formal guarantees. Drawing from graph theory, knowledge representation, specification mining, and formal verification research, we explore what makes these problems hard, where current approaches break at scale, and what architectural principles make full-lifecycle autonomy tractable. Attendees will leave with a rigorous understanding of the representation and reasoning foundations required for agents that don’t just generate code but understand, validate, evolve, and replace software as knowledge.", "language": "en"},"INV513": {"abstract": "Trace a pod from kubelet's RunPodSandbox call to process execution inside a VM isolated sandbox, then dissect the hard problems at each boundary crossing: how do you enforce policy on what runtime actions are allowed? How do you transport container images into an isolated environment the parent cannot read? Where does attestation fit, and how can you truly trust what is running inside? We examine how the trust model shifts between untrusted host and untrusted guest, explore the tradeoffs between CRI compatibility and security invariants, and discuss how EKS is approaching VM-level workload isolation", "language": "en"},"INV514": {"abstract": "As frontier models scale in capability, so does their potential for deceptive and manipulative behavior—risks that static, single-turn evaluations fail to capture. We present our multi-layered approach to measuring emergent deception under Amazon's Frontier Model Safety Framework (FMSF), combining automated benchmarks, expert red-teaming, and uplift studies. We will dive deep into two innovations: D-REX, a benchmark detecting discrepancies between benign outputs and malicious internal chain-of-thought reasoning; and LH-Deception, a multi-agent simulation revealing emergent \"chains of deception\" across extended task sequences. Together, these tools address critical gaps in frontier safety evaluation and inform deployment governance.", "language": "en"},"INV515": {"abstract": "Every time we rank an LLM, we're building a model of human preference — yet for most queries there's no ground truth, only a person's sense of which answer is better, shifting with expertise, culture, and expectations. This session examines how we model preference across two regimes: pairwise battles, arena-style voting ranked through Bradley-Terry, and agentic systems, where preference spans long-horizon, multi-step, tool-using tasks. Reward models and LLM-as-judge are themselves learned preference models; from our experience at Amazon, we examine when to trust them and how to separate progress from preference-overfitting. Attendees will learn what modeling preference at scale demands.", "language": "en"},"INV515-R": {"abstract": "Every time we rank an LLM, we're building a model of human preference — yet for most queries there's no ground truth, only a person's sense of which answer is better, shifting with expertise, culture, and expectations. This session examines how we model preference across two regimes: pairwise battles, arena-style voting ranked through Bradley-Terry, and agentic systems, where preference spans long-horizon, multi-step, tool-using tasks. Reward models and LLM-as-judge are themselves learned preference models; from our experience at Amazon, we examine when to trust them and how to separate progress from preference-overfitting. Attendees will learn what modeling preference at scale demands.", "language": "en"},"INV515-R1": {"abstract": "Every time we rank an LLM, we're building a model of human preference — yet for most queries there's no ground truth, only a person's sense of which answer is better, shifting with expertise, culture, and expectations. This session examines how we model preference across two regimes: pairwise battles, arena-style voting ranked through Bradley-Terry, and agentic systems, where preference spans long-horizon, multi-step, tool-using tasks. Reward models and LLM-as-judge are themselves learned preference models; from our experience at Amazon, we examine when to trust them and how to separate progress from preference-overfitting. Attendees will learn what modeling preference at scale demands.", "language": "en"},"INV516": {"abstract": "What does it mean, *precisely*, for the data of a virtual machine to be confidential? Answering this question will take us on a journey into low-level systems and high-level mathematics as we unpack this question in the context of the AWS Nitro System, the underlying virtualization platform that powers Amazon EC2. You will discover the low-level hardware and software that isolate virtual machines, our precise mathematical definition of confidentiality, and the methods we use to establish this crucial property for the AWS Nitro Isolation Engine. No background in virtualization systems or formal verification is expected.", "language": "en"},"INV517": {"abstract": "Disaggregated inference separates prefill and decode onto distinct GPU pools, but creates new problems. Head-of-line blocking on prefill, stragglers on decode, and pipeline bubbles when phases switch. This session goes deep on how to solve them. We cover temporally-disaggregated pipeline parallelism to eliminate phase-switching bubbles, adaptive scheduling across heterogeneous instances, tensor vs. pipeline parallelism tradeoffs for latency and throughput, constrained decoding with context-sensitive grammars for structured output without sacrificing token throughput, and KV-cache parallelism for multi-million context serving.", "language": "en"},"INV518": {"abstract": "Quantum computers have the potential to solve some of today's most challenging computational problems — but they suffer from significantly higher error rates than their classical counterparts. In this talk, we introduce quantum error correction (QEC) and compare the hardware requirements of leading QEC methods against the commercial roadmaps of quantum hardware companies. To ground the discussion, we begin with a brief revisit of classical error correction before walking through recent breakthroughs in quantum error correction. Attendees will leave with an informed perspective on where QEC stands today and the challenges that will define the field's progress in the coming years.", "language": "en"},"INV520": {"abstract": "As organizations deploy generative AI at scale, watermarking has become the critical trust layer for establishing content provenance, meeting emerging regulatory requirements, and protecting brand integrity. But what if an adversary could stamp a fake watermark onto any image and frame your service? We show a diffusion-based forgery attack bypasses current defenses with just 1,000 samples. To close this gap, we introduce a self-verifiable message mechanism—SeedBind and ContentBind—that transforms verification into a structural consistency check, reducing forged bit accuracy to near-random without modifying the underlying watermarking pipeline. Discover how to harden AI provenance at scale.", "language": "en"},"INV521": {"abstract": "An AI agent investigating an incident starts cold: no sense of normal, no topology, no causal history. It rebuilds context from raw documents every time. What if the telemetry understood itself first? We'll examine an intelligence layer for agentic observability, where streaming detectors and a live service graph distill billions of signals into incident observations, then fine-tuned reasoning models, built on open-weight LLMs, correlate them into ranked root causes. Whiteboard with us on the hard parts: what to teach a model versus retrieve at inference, how domain fine-tuning beats frontier scale, and where reasoning models still fail.", "language": "en"},"INV521-R": {"abstract": "An AI agent investigating an incident starts cold: no sense of normal, no topology, no causal history. It rebuilds context from raw documents every time. What if the telemetry understood itself first? We'll examine an intelligence layer for agentic observability, where streaming detectors and a live service graph distill billions of signals into incident observations, then fine-tuned reasoning models, built on open-weight LLMs, correlate them into ranked root causes. Whiteboard with us on the hard parts: what to teach a model versus retrieve at inference, how domain fine-tuning beats frontier scale, and where reasoning models still fail.", "language": "en"},"INV521-R1": {"abstract": "An AI agent investigating an incident starts cold: no sense of normal, no topology, no causal history. It rebuilds context from raw documents every time. What if the telemetry understood itself first? We'll examine an intelligence layer for agentic observability, where streaming detectors and a live service graph distill billions of signals into incident observations, then fine-tuned reasoning models, built on open-weight LLMs, correlate them into ranked root causes. Whiteboard with us on the hard parts: what to teach a model versus retrieve at inference, how domain fine-tuning beats frontier scale, and where reasoning models still fail.", "language": "en"},"INV522": {"abstract": "We discovered a phase transition in LLM-guided code evolution: when the evaluation function provides smooth gradient signal, SOTA emerges automatically regardless of model choice; when it doesn't, no compute compensates. This \"evaluator-dominance principle\" emerged from stress-testing evolutionary search across 403 problems in 27 domains on Amazon Bedrock — 20× the scale of Google's original AlphaEvolve — achieving 96% SOTA-matching at $15/problem median cost. We'll present the Bedrock-native architecture (MAP-Elites, staged evaluation cascade, Opus/Haiku phase routing), a production vehicle routing deployment saving 1.28% on 100,000-customer routes at $530 total spend, and the evaluator design methodology that determines success or failure.", "language": "en"},"INV523": {"abstract": "Your consumers are healthy, your pods are green - yet your backlog isn't shrinking. Why? Because systems provisioned for steady-state have zero recovery capacity. In this chalk talk, we'll derive the formulas that govern backlog formation and drain, explore the non-linear utilization cliff where small traffic spikes become catastrophic, and work through the real-world complications: retry amplification, stale-message degradation, and multi-stage cascades. Bring your architectures - we'll whiteboard whether they can actually recover from failure, or just survive it.", "language": "en"},"INV524": {"abstract": "Traditionally, cryptographic engineering involves a performance/assurance trade-off: clean code is easy to audit and maintain, yet slow; handwritten assembly is fast, yet hard to validate. Discover how automated reasoning helps to overcome this tension, following the example of mlkem-native and mldsa-native, two open-source post-quantum cryptography libraries co-maintained by AWS and deployed in AWS-LC, Amazon's cryptography library. In these libraries, constraint solving enables automated superoptimization at the assembly level, while formal verification vets the result. Contrary to the popular belief that assurance costs speed, it is automated reasoning that enables deep optimization, verification and rapid deployment of cryptographic code at cloud scale.", "language": "en"},"INV525": {"abstract": "Journey into the sophisticated virtualization architecture that powers AgentCore's runtime security model. This session examines the intricate mechanics of session isolation at the virtualization layer, exploring advanced techniques in compute and memory virtualization, virtual machine snapshot optimization, and virtual memory deduplication. Drawing from operating systems research and systems security principles, we'll demonstrate how these components work together to create robust security boundaries for agent workloads. Attendees will gain deep technical insights into the mathematical and systems-level concepts that position AWS at the forefront of secure agent runtime architectures, showcasing our industry-leading approach to securing code execution in agent environments.", "language": "en"},"INV526": {"abstract": "AI agents that write and execute code autonomously create a fundamental trust problem: how do you guarantee behavioral boundaries without constraining the flexibility that makes agents useful? This chalk talk examines how neurosymbolic AI is making provable guarantees practical at scale: autoformalization translating natural-language constraints into temporal logic, satisfiability solvers whose exponential improvement keeps pace with exponentially harder verification problems, and LLMs guiding proof search in theorem provers like Lean, creating a virtuous cycle where better proofs yield stronger provers. We'll stratify what can be proved today, what requires LLM-discovered inductive invariants, and where undecidability still draws a hard line.", "language": "en"},"INV527": {"abstract": "World models promise to give robots predictive understanding of their environment, yet applying them to indoor robotics exposes fundamental gaps in how transformers reason about geometry. In this session, we present findings from building a trajectory-conditioned lidar world model for Diligent Robotics' hospital fleet on AWS. We examine why standard loss metrics fail for generative spatial predictions, why rotation breaks factored attention even with explicit conditioning, and the minimal set of inductive biases needed to restore geometric coherence. Attendees leave with a diagnostic framework for spatial reasoning failures in transformer-based world models.", "language": "en"},"INV528": {"abstract": "Preventing long outages, meta-stable events, thundering herds, Denial of Service Attacks, resource or concurrency starvation, or noisy neighbor issues all depend on fairness and admission control. We’ll do a deep, interactive dive into practical techniques that prevent or mitigate real-world issues. We’ll get into details of back-pressure, PID control loops, shuffle sharding, floodgates, and other novel techniques that we’ve developed at AWS, and we’ll cover common mistakes that we see in the industry.", "language": "en"},"INV529": {"abstract": "RSA and Elliptic Curve Cryptography secure nearly every application today, and both are vulnerable to future cryptographically relevant quantum computers. Data captured now can be decrypted later, so applications needing long-term confidentiality should adopt post-quantum encryption well before those computers arrive. AWS KMS now offers ML-KEM, the NIST-standardized replacement for RSA and ECDH key establishment and ultimately encryption under asymmetric keys. It protects the private keys in FIPS 140-3 Level 3 HSMs. In this session, learn about ML-KEM and how to use it as a building block for quantum-safe encryption using Hybrid Public Key Encryption (HPKE).", "language": "en"},"INV530": {"abstract": "Amazon EC2 launched in 2006 as four services backed by a single MySQL database. Twenty years later, it is one of the largest distributed systems ever built — and most of that original architecture had to be replaced while serving production traffic, with no maintenance window and no change to the API contract customers depend on. In this session, EC2 engineers dive deep on two of the hardest migrations: making the control plane Availability Zone independent so a single-AZ event cannot escalate into a regional one, and moving EC2 metadata off MySQL. We walk through the failure modes that forced each decision, how correctness was verified before every cutover, how we proved a single-AZ failure stays in a single AZ, and the things we got wrong along the way. You will leave with migration and isolation patterns you can apply to your own systems at any scale.", "language": "en"},"INV530-R": {"abstract": "Amazon EC2 launched in 2006 as four services backed by a single MySQL database. Twenty years later, it is one of the largest distributed systems ever built — and most of that original architecture had to be replaced while serving production traffic, with no maintenance window and no change to the API contract customers depend on. In this session, EC2 engineers dive deep on two of the hardest migrations: making the control plane Availability Zone independent so a single-AZ event cannot escalate into a regional one, and moving EC2 metadata off MySQL. We walk through the failure modes that forced each decision, how correctness was verified before every cutover, how we proved a single-AZ failure stays in a single AZ, and the things we got wrong along the way. You will leave with migration and isolation patterns you can apply to your own systems at any scale.", "language": "en"},"INV530-R1": {"abstract": "Amazon EC2 launched in 2006 as four services backed by a single MySQL database. Twenty years later, it is one of the largest distributed systems ever built — and most of that original architecture had to be replaced while serving production traffic, with no maintenance window and no change to the API contract customers depend on. In this session, EC2 engineers dive deep on two of the hardest migrations: making the control plane Availability Zone independent so a single-AZ event cannot escalate into a regional one, and moving EC2 metadata off MySQL. We walk through the failure modes that forced each decision, how correctness was verified before every cutover, how we proved a single-AZ failure stays in a single AZ, and the things we got wrong along the way. You will leave with migration and isolation patterns you can apply to your own systems at any scale.", "language": "en"},"INV531": {"abstract": "Today’s vision-language models can describe images fluently, yet still struggle with the spatial and perceptual reasoning required for robotics. This talk argues that visual reasoning—reasoning through points, sketches, trajectories, depth maps, and other grounded representations—will become more important than language reasoning for physical agents. It traces progress from benchmarks exposing fundamental perception failures to methods that teach models to reason directly in visual space. These ideas culminate in robotics foundation models that convert visual plans into robot actions. The resulting systems are interpretable, steerable, deployable zero-shot on affordable hardware, and competitive with proprietary models trained on substantially more data. We showcase how these ideas are advancing the goal of accessible, general-purpose robotics to the broader research community.", "language": "en"},"INV533": {"abstract": "Reflecting on my roles as Head of Computer Science at MIT, Director of the MIT Education Exch(AI)nge, and Chief Scientist at Cambridge Mobile Telematics, I will discuss how the rise of generative AI is reshaping software, computer science itself, higher education, and the ways data- and AI-driven companies create value from data. In computer science, we are seeing explosive growth both in the range of problems AI can solve and in the new systems and tools required to make AI effective, efficient, and reliable. In higher education, we are rethinking fundamental questions about what students need to learn, as well as how we teach and assess our students. At CMT, we are applying new AI architectures to better understand mobility data, particularly road and driver risk. I will use concrete examples and results to explore the common theme of how to achieve the greatest impact from AI.", "language": "en"},"INV534": {"abstract": "The \"Software Factory\" is a new buzzword for a fully autonomous agent driven software development process. Many organizations are trying to set up such a factory, but this is easier said than done. In this talk, from my experience setting up such factories for ourselves and other businesses, I will explain some of the elements of a software factory (issue triage, spec generation, implementation, review, and testing), and some of the pain points that we have experienced at each step. I will also cover how to evaluate the effectiveness of each of these steps and how to build a self-improvement pipeline so the factory gets better and better with feedback from its human supervisors.", "language": "en"},"INV535": {"abstract": "AI agents increasingly query databases, modify files, and operate applications. Their effectiveness depends on the environments in which they act: what information they can discover, which alternatives they can explore, and what happens when they make mistakes. This session examines how data environments can help agents accomplish more while bounding the consequences of failure. Drawing on research from Columbia’s Data, Agents, and Processes Lab, we explore branching and recovery across databases and live execution state, data flow controls that enforce policies and guide agent behavior, and evaluations that reveal bottlenecks in finding and using information. Participants will leave with a framework for reasoning about the responsibilities of models, execution systems, and human interfaces when building agentic applications.", "language": "en"},"INV536": {"abstract": "This talk will discuss RSI (recursive self-improvement) agents that proactively and continuously learn on the job. Agents onboard a new knowledge job just like human employees. Through RSI, we show that agents can outperform human testers on the same job across accuracy, efficiency, and cost. The talk will end with a discussion on the future of human-AI collaboration.", "language": "en"},"INV537": {"abstract": "How do you make a humanoid robot safe to operate alongside people, without the torque sensors? Fauna Robotics Sprout is a humanoid robot that uses a position-command interface, yet it must regulate the forces it exchanges with the world. We show how Sprout learns compliant behavior that approximates classic impedance control, through mechanisms that control force at the joints and in task space. We describe how these controllers are trained and how they enable safer, whole-body interaction.", "language": "en"},"INV538": {"abstract": "At AWS, we're constantly looking for ways to automate parts of the building process with agents. An upcoming launch takes this to the extreme, releasing an application that was built entirely by autonomous agents without a human ever touching the code base. The agents built a first version over a weekend. But turning that into a robust application was a much longer journey than we expected. In this talk, we pull back the curtain on this upcoming launch and talk about the machine that builds the machine.", "language": "en"},"INV539": {"abstract": "Next-token prediction has been effective for language, but is it the right learning objective for achieving clinical intelligence from longitudinal human health data? In this talk, we show that treating health records as flat token sequences limit the advancement of foundation models in healthcare. We then introduce a Health State–Action World Model that abstracts longitudinal records into learned representations of patient health states and clinical actions, and models how these states evolve under different interventions. This abstraction enables pretraining models to predict future health events, diagnoses, treatments, and responses to treatment. Across large-scale datasets, we show that moving from token-level prediction to state–action modeling enables clinical intelligence that cannot be achieved through token prediction alone, while generalizing strongly to unseen patients.", "language": "en"},"JPN101": {"abstract": "This session will be delivered in Japanese and provides a comprehensive recap of the major announcements and strategic updates from the keynote sessions throughout the event. Experienced speakers will summarize the most impactful innovations and key messages from AWS leadership. Designed for a broad audience, from developers and engineers to business decision-makers, this session will help you identify and prioritize the announcements most relevant to your organization. It will also help you prepare to share what you learned at re:Invent with your teams when you return to the office", "language": "en"},"KEY001": {"abstract": "Building software has never moved faster, and it has never asked more of developers. In this keynote, AWS Engineers and Heroes from the community tackle what's still hard: going from idea to a live app in minutes, building agents that actually work, and trusting code you didn't write yourself. Expect live demos, hard-won lessons from the AWS team that built a new service with an AI software factory, and an honest look at how the developer's job is changing. Come see how AWS is making it easier to build anything you can imagine, then go build it.", "language": "en"},"KEY002": {"abstract": "Join us for the CEO Keynote at AWS re:Invent 2026. Hear the latest news and announcements, insights from AWS leaders, and stories from customers building what's next. Full session details coming soon.", "language": "en"},"KEY003": {"abstract": "Join us for the Data & AI Keynote at AWS re:Invent 2026. Hear the latest news and announcements, insights from AWS leaders, and stories from customers building what's next. Full session details coming soon.", "language": "en"},"KEY004": {"abstract": "AWS Partners, this one's for you. You're powering today's biggest transformations, and AWS is building alongside you to make co-selling and going to market simpler. This is your biggest moment of re:Invent, where you'll leave knowing where to invest and how to win in the agentic era. Join Dr. Ruba Borno and guests including AWS CEO Matt Garman for a game plan built on agent-run migrations with your data plugged in, your expertise embedded in customer tools, and more profitable business models, plus a Marketplace and Partner Central demo on reaching more buyers faster. Don't miss it.", "language": "en"},"KEY005": {"abstract": "Agents don't follow a script. They change plans mid-task, reach for tools they didn't know they'd need, and create demand you can't predict. For two decades, the hardest workloads builders brought to AWS have pushed us to rethink everything from Graviton and Trainium to the shape of the data center network, to the way storage scales, and how we prove systems behave as intended. Join AWS's top engineers and scientists for an under-the-hood look at the technology that came out of that pressure, the kind of story you won't hear anywhere else. See how AWS infrastructure adapts as your work changes, freeing you to stop engineering around constraints and build anything you can imagine.", "language": "en"},"MAM101-S": {"abstract": "Modernizing critical systems is never just about technology. In this executive panel, leaders from Aflac, Kyndryl, and AWS share the real-world experience behind Aflac’s transformation journey, from overcoming operational challenges and reducing downtime risk to enabling future innovation & continuous modernization. Learn how a strong customer-partner relationship helped balance transformation with business continuity, security, and regulatory requirements. Gain insights into managing large-scale transformation, avoiding common pitfalls, and building a roadmap that delivers measurable business value while preparing for the next generation of cloud and AI-driven innovation. This presentation is brought to you by Kyndryl, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"}};
  window.APP_DATA.sessions.forEach(function (s) {
    var x = m[s.session_id];
    if (x) for (var k in x) if (x[k]) s[k] = x[k];
  });
})();
